  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "The `{cite}` of `requirements.authoring.invocation` on `enum[AssessmentKind]` (src/authoring/inputs.rs:14-19) is a gap. It should be removed.\n\n- **What the requirement says.** It specifies the `habeas author <full-name> [--config] [--missing|--force] [--stdout] [-v]` command. That command authors evidence for one existing requirement or example. It must report the subject, backend, model, evidence destination, and usage-record path. It must send the report and diagnostics to stderr in stdout mode. It must keep `author-evidence` as an alias.\n- **What the item is.** The enum has two variants, `Definition(DefinitionKind)` and `Citation(CitationItem)`. It only tags whether an assessment is a definition review or a citation review. It has no code that parses arguments, checks flags, or builds a report or destination. It does not read anything from the invocation text.\n- **Implementation test.** The enum does not implement any part of the requirement. The code that does so is `AssessmentSources::prepare` (cited with `{impl}` for the invocation, at src/authoring/inputs.rs:50-206) and the command front end.\n- **Cite test.** I looked for a reason a change to the invocation text would force a change to this enum. Changing the flags, the completion report, the stdout routing, or the alias would not touch it. The enum's own `{impl}` of `requirements.references.citation_review` already explains why it exists. Its `Definition` variant only loosely corresponds to \"one requirement or named example\".\n\nSo the citation calls for no reference at all.",
  "inputs": [
